From: ReadyNAS NG <readynasng@build>
Subject: [PATCH 29/30] padre_gmac: TCP segmentation by the engine (TSO)

The GMT engine of the GMAC segments TCP by itself (command bit 7, already
part of the resting command 0xc0 sent since 0023): a TCP frame whose IP
packet is longer than GMT0_MSS (1500, written at open like 2.6.17) leaves
as packets of 1500 bytes. Seen in MAC loopback (tso_verify, below) on
frames shaped like the stack's GSO frames (IP length and checksum of the
whole frame, 12 bytes of TCP options, ACK+PSH+FIN, 4445 to 60000 bytes,
buffers of up to 55 KiB at odd addresses): the payload per segment follows
the TCP header length (1448 with timestamps), IP ids count up from the
frame's, IP and TCP checksums are right (the engine computes the
pseudo-header itself), sequence numbers follow, PSH and FIN only on the
last segment, data and every other header field unchanged.

The engine takes no MSS per frame (2.6.17 compared the length with the
global tcp_mtu only), so ndo_features_check leaves GSO to the stack unless
headers + gso_size make exactly 1500 bytes, the frame is TCP over IPv4
without IP options, the engine is trusted (eng_ok) and the link runs at
1000 Mbit/s (2.6.17 allowed TSO at that speed only). A GSO frame reaching
gm_xmit after the engine was turned off is dropped (TCP sends it again,
segmented by the stack); the fallback of 0023 turns TSO off with the
checksum. NETIF_F_TSO only: no TSO_ECN (CWR on later segments), no IPv6.
tso_max (read at boot, default 64 KiB) caps the frame handed over.

Off by default: padre_gmac.tso=1, the same parameter in
/sys/module/padre_gmac/parameters at run time, or ethtool -K eth0 tso on.
ethtool -S: tx_tso, tx_tso_drop. tso_verify (echo 1 > .../tso_verify, link
up, the network stops for a few seconds) runs the loopback experiment and
logs each segment of the first frame.

Tried on the NAS (Wi-Fi PC): segments captured whole on the PC (GRO off
for the capture), every TCP checksum correct, IP ids consecutive,
1500-byte segments, no IP header or checksum error counted by the PC;
500 MiB over raw TCP (sendfile) and NFSv3 reads byte for byte, TSO off/on
interleaved: NAS CPU 0.049-0.052 -> 0.035-0.046 CPU s/MiB (TCP),
0.047-0.052 -> 0.038-0.046 (NFS), 18-25 times fewer frames through the
driver, throughput bound by the Wi-Fi either way.

Still off by default: data sent through write() (arbitrary buffer shapes)
was compared byte for byte only once, SSH and SMB not at all; and the
tx_guard of 0023 turned the engine off once on a trial with TSO on, while
the PC's Wi-Fi was failing (300-800 ms round trips).
